<h1 class="article-title">Proposals collected for new street names in Liepāja Industrial Park</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>At the ‘Made in Liepāja’ exhibition earlier this year, the Liepāja SEZ Authority invited residents to take part in a historic initiative: proposing names for eight new streets being built on the site of the former Liepājas metalurgs steelworks. On 26 May, the exhibition results were symbolically presented at Liepāja Industrial Park, with potential locations for Martena, Trīs skursteņu, Inženieru, Eksporta, Čuguna, Naglu, Dūmu and Dzelzsgriezēja streets marked on a map. The collected proposals will be submitted to Liepāja City Municipality, which will make the final decision on the street names.</p><p>From 30 January to 1 February, every visitor to the ‘Made in Liepāja’ exhibition could write down a proposed street name and pin it to a specially prepared map. In total, 385 suggestions were received, demonstrating local residents’ interest in shaping the city’s development. ‘Most respondents believed that street names should preserve something of Liepāja’s industrial history—such as Martena Street, Čuguna Street, Naglu Street and Trīs skursteņu Street. Many residents also want Liepāja to be a green, clean and sustainable city, which is why the proposed Dūmu Street gained support too—so that smoke remains only in history and in a street name,’ says Liepāja SEZ CEO Uldis Hmieļevskis. A proposal by Minister for Economics Viktors Valainis to name one of the streets Eksporta Street also attracted considerable support.</p><p>Exhibition participants particularly welcomed the suggestion of Dzelzsgriezēja Street, highlighting the city’s industrial heritage and its ability to transform while looking to the future. Notably, the legendary Latvian rock band Līvi began its musical journey in 1976 at the Liepājas metalurgs Palace of Culture, a place where industry and creativity came together. Long-time band member Ainars Virga attended the event, underscoring the site’s special significance for Liepāja’s cultural history.</p><p>The Liepāja SEZ Authority thanks all exhibition visitors for their creativity and active participation!</p><p> </p><p>For information:</p><p>Liepāja Industrial Park is being developed on the site of the former Liepājas metalurgs steelworks. A local spatial plan has been prepared for the area of more than 120 hectares, providing for public access and the construction of eight new streets.</p><p>The Liepāja SEZ Authority and Liepāja City Municipality are developing Liepāja Industrial Park on the former steelworks site between Brīvības, Meldru, Ezermalas, Parka and Zemnieku streets. The site borders Lake Liepāja, a specially protected Natura 2000 area. Under the approved development strategy, the plan is to attract more than EUR 275 million in private investment and create at least 1,500 new jobs.</p><p>Timeline:</p><ul><li>In 2018, the Ministry of Economics, Investment and Development Agency of Latvia, FeLM SIA, Liepāja City Council and Liepāja SEZ Authority signed a memorandum of intent concerning the development of the former Liepājas metalurgs site.</li><li>In 2021, the development programme for the former Liepājas metalurgs site was approved, and an interinstitutional agreement on its implementation was concluded with Liepāja City Council.</li><li>In 2022, the local spatial plan for the former Liepājas metalurgs site was approved.</li><li>In 2022, a remediation programme was prepared for the former Liepājas metalurgs site.</li><li>In 2023, the Liepāja Industrial Park (LIP) Development Strategy for 2023–2028 was approved.</li><li>In 2023, several construction designs were prepared for infrastructure development works in the area.</li><li>In 2023, an application for the first street construction works in Liepāja Industrial Park was prepared and submitted, and a favourable decision on funding was received.</li><li>In 2023, a lease agreement was signed with Liepājas Steel SIA, valid until 31 December 2025, for the dismantling and removal of the electric steel-melting furnace.</li></ul><p> </p></div>
